---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { themes, totalQuestions, syllabus, source, u, issueUrl, telegramBot } from '../lib/data';
const liveTutor = Boolean(import.meta.env.PUBLIC_STAGE0_API_URL);
---
<Base title="About and sources" description="How UPSC Themes was built: sources of the UPSC questions, how themes were formed, known gaps, and how to report an error." crumbs={[{ name: 'About', href: u('/about/') }]}>
  <h1>About UPSC Themes</h1>
  <p>A free, non-commercial study aid for UPSC Civil Services aspirants. It regroups {totalQuestions} GS1, GS2 and GS3 Mains questions (2013–2026) into {themes.length} themes so related topics from different papers can be prepared together.</p>
  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Questions: Civil Services (Main) Examination papers published by the <a href={source.official} rel="noopener">Union Public Service Commission</a>. Copyright, if any, remains with UPSC.</li>
    <li>Prelims questions (PYQs of the day): Civil Services (Preliminary) GS Paper I, 2013–2026. Answers follow the published answer keys. UPSC releases its official key only after the final result, so 2026 answers are not yet final. Questions UPSC dropped are marked and left out of the daily sets.</li>
    <li>Syllabus: the GS Mains syllabus in the CSE notification ({syllabus.length} lines across GS1–GS3).</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>How themes were formed</h2>
  <p>The theme map and prep notes are this site's own work. Each question was read against the syllabus and placed in the one theme it fits best. Where answering it well needs another theme, that theme is linked on the question ("Also needs") or on the theme ("Linked themes"). Counts, years and directive words are computed from the data.</p>
  <h2>Known gaps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>2019 is missing for GS1 and GS3; GS2 2019 has 10 questions.</li>
    <li>Question text is being checked against the official papers. Some entries may still carry typos from the compilation they came from.</li>
    <li>Marks are missing for most questions before 2022.</li>
  </ul>
  {issueUrl() && <>
  <h2>Report an error</h2>
  <p>Every question has a "Report a typo" link that opens a GitHub issue with its ID. You can also <a href={issueUrl()} rel="noopener" data-umami-event="report-error" data-umami-event-where="about">open an issue directly</a>.</p>
  </>}
  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>This site counts visits and clicks with Umami, which uses no cookies and collects no personal data. Browsers that send "Do Not Track" are not counted.</p>
  <h3 id="stage0-privacy">Concept Explorer learning data</h3>
  {liveTutor ? <>
    <p>The concept catalogue and reusable learning prompts can be read without an account. If you create a learning account, the tutor service stores your username, password and recovery-code hashes, conversations, understanding-check answers and drafts, progress and revisit reminders. A secure HttpOnly session cookie keeps you signed in.</p>
    <p>When you use the AI tutor, your question and recent conversation context, or your check answer and lesson context, are sent to the configured AI provider for a reply or feedback. Avoid sharing personal information. AI processing follows the provider’s own policies; the app does not promise zero provider retention.</p>
    <p>You can download your saved learning data or permanently delete your account through “Manage your learning data” on a Concept Explorer page. Deletion removes active account records; existing hosting backups may retain earlier copies until rotated. Application request receipts are retained for seven days and operational rate counters for eight days. Rate limiting uses keyed IP hashes rather than storing raw IP addresses.</p>
  </> : <p>The current Concept Explorer preview saves its sample conversations and progress in your browser. Its sample replies do not send your input to an AI provider. You can clear this preview data in your browser’s site-storage settings. A separately configured live tutor uses accounts and explains its data handling before sign-in.</p>}
  <p>Daily PYQs on Telegram: when you press Start in <a href={telegramBot}>our Telegram bot</a>, it keeps only your Telegram chat ID, stored encrypted, so it can send you the daily set. It does not keep your name, phone number or messages. Send /stop to unsubscribe and your chat ID is deleted.</p>
</Base>
